{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 for a Dream review of a simulated ML paper. Your scope is **Method + Experiments** only. Do NOT review novelty or related work.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"When Hard Negatives Hurt: Embedding-Distance Negative Mining Degrades Contrastive Code Retrieval\"\n\nThe paper challenges the assumption that hard negative mining improves contrastive learning for code retrieval. It shows embedding-distance hard negatives DEGRADE retrieval quality vs random negatives (-5.0pp R@5, p=0.003 on SWE-bench Lite). The mechanism is \"gradient redundancy\" — negatives close in embedding space reinforce existing similarity judgments rather than teaching new distinctions (Pearson r=0.71). They propose structure-grounded negatives using CodeQL data-flow connectivity, which match random performance (+0.7pp, p=0.42) without the embedding-distance pathologies. A ceiling analysis on 50 failures shows only 22% are addressable by structural signal, bounding gains to ~3-4pp.\n\n## Key Details for Your Review\n\n**Problem Setup (§3.1):** File-level code retrieval for repo-level bug repair. Query = bug report + stack trace, repository files ranked by relevance. Gold = files modified in ground-truth patch. Bi-encoder with InfoNCE loss.\n\n**Three strategies compared (§3.2):**\n- Random: k negatives sampled uniformly from non-gold files\n- Embedding-distance (EmbDist): k non-gold files with highest cosine similarity to query, re-mined every epoch\n- Data-flow-aware (DataFlow): textually similar (BM25 > τ) but structurally disconnected files via CodeQL\n\n**Structural graph construction (§3.3):** CodeQL with 2-hop call-graph bounding. Z% = fraction of instances with connected-but-not-gold files > 0. Z% drops from 40.8% (50-repo pilot) to 25.0% (5K full scale).\n\n**Model (§3.4):** Qwen2.5-Coder-3B with LoRA, InfoNCE, temp 0.05, batch 32, lr 2e-5, cosine schedule, 3 epochs, 3 seeds (42, 123, 456), 5000 SWE-smith instances.\n\n**Results:**\n- Table 3 (SWE-bench Lite): Random R@5=.810±.012, DataFlow R@5=.817±.010, EmbDist R@5=.760±.012\n- Table 4: Paired t-test p-values: EmbDist-Random p=0.003, DataFlow-Random p=0.42\n- Table 5 (RepoBench-R): StructFuse-Random R@5=.618, StructFuse-DataFlow=.625, StructFuse-EmbDist=.592\n- Table 6 (end-to-end SWE-bench Lite resolve): Random 28.3%, DataFlow 28.7%, EmbDist 27.8%, BM25-only 26.1%\n- Table 7: Embedding-distance harm by repo size — medium repos worst (-6.8pp)\n- Table 8: Harm scales with cosine similarity of negatives (r=0.71). Q4 (most similar, mean 0.91): -9.1pp; Q1 (least, 0.61): -1.5pp\n- Table 9: Ceiling analysis on 50 failures — only 22% addressable by data-flow\n- Table 10: Hop ablation — 2-hop Pareto-optimal (Z%=25%, timeout 7.8%, R@5=.817)\n- Table 11: Training data scaling — log-linear R²=0.96, diminishing returns at 5K\n- Table 12: Fusion head ablation — structural features add only +0.5pp at inference\n\n**This is a \"median\" tier dream paper — evaluate with typical standards.**\n\n## Your Review Dimensions\n1. **Method description**: Is it complete? Are claims supported by experimental data? Are key hyperparameters and training procedures clear?\n2. **Experimental design**: Does it cover key baselines, ablations, cross-dataset, and scale dimensions?\n3. **Data presentation**: Can a reviewer reproduce conclusions from tables/figures? Are error bars and statistical significance sufficient?\n\nPlease provide your review in structured format with: Strengths (bulleted), Weaknesses (bulleted, numbered W1, W2...), Questions for Authors, and a preliminary rating (strong reject / reject / borderline / weak accept / accept / strong accept).\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01E1ixM8X4KHPMKRUeMLDaoG","session_id":"701cd20f-444e-4df2-ad65-972d8dd61bb4","uuid":"53e052d8-047a-4c72-9fd4-d8798625dff0","timestamp":"2026-05-12T21:16:01.021Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01LCMxrdHnEWCegXgQHD6hth","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019MGd4JP75uYoBRxknXNgbc","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 for a Dream review of a simulated ML paper. Your scope is **Novelty + Related Work + Story Consistency** only. Do NOT review method implementation details or experimental design.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"When Hard Negatives Hurt: Embedding-Distance Negative Mining Degrades Contrastive Code Retrieval\"\n\nThe paper challenges the assumption that hard negative mining improves contrastive learning for code retrieval. Key claims:\n1. Embedding-distance hard negatives significantly harm contrastive code retrieval (-5.0pp R@5 on SWE-bench Lite, -2.6pp on RepoBench-R)\n2. The mechanism is \"gradient redundancy\" — negatives close in embedding space reinforce existing similarity judgments, with Pearson r=0.71 between cosine similarity and performance degradation\n3. Data-flow-grounded negatives (using CodeQL inter-procedural connectivity) match random-negative performance (+0.7pp, p=0.42) without the embedding-distance pathologies\n4. Ceiling analysis: only 22% of retrieval failures are addressable by structural signal, bounding achievable gain to ~3-4pp\n\n**This is a \"median\" tier dream paper.**\n\n## Related Work Section (§2)\nThree subsections:\n- **Hard negative mining in contrastive learning**: Cites FaceNet (Schroff et al. 2015), DPR (Karpukhin et al. 2020), ANCE (Xiong et al. 2021), CoCoHaNeRe (Shi et al. 2023). Notes CoCoHaNeRe also found embedding-distance negatives can be redundant — \"our work extends with controlled experiments and a mechanistic explanation.\"\n- **Code retrieval for software engineering**: SweRank (Zhang et al. 2025), SWE-Fixer (Xie et al. 2025), LocAgent (Muhammad et al. 2024), RANGER, CodexGraph, ARISE (Wu et al. 2024). Claims to be \"first to use inter-procedural data-flow connectivity as a training signal for negative mining.\"\n- **Structural analysis in code understanding**: CodeQL (2024). Notes prior work uses call graphs for bug detection but not for contrastive learning data curation.\n\n## Contributions Claimed\n1. Cautionary finding: embedding-distance hard negatives harm code retrieval\n2. Safe alternative: data-flow negatives match random without pathologies\n3. Scalability: CodeQL scales to 5K repos with <8% timeout\n4. Ceiling analysis: only 22% of failures addressable by structural signal\n\n## Story Arc\n- Abstract: \"harder negatives are not always better\" — cautionary tale\n- Intro: presents gradient redundancy as mechanism, structure-grounded mining as safe alternative\n- Discussion: recommends reconsidering embedding-distance practice, identifies three bottlenecks for structure gains (low Z%, limited addressability, random near-optimal at high pool diversity)\n- Conclusion: cautionary result, actionable future directions\n\n## Limitations Stated\n1. Single encoder architecture (Qwen2.5-Coder-3B)\n2. CodeQL limited to Python\n3. Ceiling analysis based on 50 manually annotated instances\n4. End-to-end resolve rates show <1pp differences\n\n## References (11 total)\nGuo et al. 2022 (UniXcoder), Jimenez et al. 2024 (SWE-bench), Schroff et al. 2015 (FaceNet), Karpukhin et al. 2020 (DPR), Xiong et al. 2021 (ANCE), Shi et al. 2023 (CoCoHaNeRe), Zhang et al. 2025 (SweRank), Xie et al. 2025 (SWE-Fixer), Muhammad et al. 2024 (LocAgent), Wu et al. 2024 (ARISE), CodeQL 2024\n\n## Your Review Dimensions\n1. **Novelty**: Is the differentiation from existing work clear? Do the contribution points stand independently?\n2. **Related work**: Is literature coverage sufficient? Are important baselines or prior findings missing? Is positioning accurate?\n3. **Story consistency**: Is the argumentation logic self-consistent across abstract/intro/method/experiments/conclusion? Do claims in the abstract match what the experiments actually show?\n\nPlease provide your review in structured format with: Strengths (bulleted), Weaknesses (bulleted, numbered W1, W2...), Questions for Authors, and a preliminary rating (strong reject / reject / borderline / weak accept / accept / strong accept).\n","model":"sonnet"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":205,"cache_read_input_tokens":52120,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":205},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"701cd20f-444e-4df2-ad65-972d8dd61bb4","uuid":"2eb4c7f1-aa05-4753-be7e-7f2d21c517bc"}
