{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01QyG6mmYVZhPGr5LjUTxyLk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011cXxQuv4vaBMRkfkV7Pdq1","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1 cross-share on R2 findings","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (Method + Experiments scope) for a Dream paper review. You previously reviewed \"When Hard Negatives Hurt: Embedding-Distance Negative Mining Degrades Contrastive Code Retrieval\" and gave a \"Borderline (leaning weak reject)\" rating.\n\nYour key weaknesses were:\n- W1: Z% coverage collapse (40.8%→25.0%) not explained\n- W2: EmbDist re-mining every epoch is a confound (curriculum shift vs selection criterion)\n- W3: InfoNCE temperature τ=0.05 not ablated, could interact with negative hardness\n- W4: End-to-end resolve rate gap (0.5pp) is ~10× smaller than R@5 gap (5.0pp)\n- W5: Gradient redundancy is correlation (r=0.71), not actual gradient measurement\n- W6: RepoBench-R results lack error bars\n\nNow Reviewer 2 (Novelty + Related Work) has submitted their report. From your Method+Experiments perspective, provide supplementary comments on R2's findings:\n\n**R2's Weaknesses:**\n- W1: Positioning against CoCoHaNeRe insufficient — unclear if gradient redundancy mechanism is novel or restated\n- W2: \"First to use inter-procedural data-flow\" overclaimed — GraphCodeBERT uses data-flow edges in pre-training, clone detection literature not surveyed\n- W3: Reference list sparse (11 entries) — missing Kalantidis et al. 2020, Robinson et al. 2021, GraphCodeBERT, RocketQA, ADORE\n- W4: Story consistency — abstract claims domain-general mechanism but conclusion narrows to single encoder/Python\n- W5: DataFlow result (+0.7pp, p=0.42) framed as \"safe alternative\" but is statistically a null result\n\n**Your task:** From your method+experiments angle, comment on:\n1. Does R2's concern about CoCoHaNeRe (their W1) affect the methodological contribution? Is the controlled experiment design sufficient differentiation even if the mechanism was previously noted?\n2. Does R2's concern about GraphCodeBERT (their W2) impact the experimental baselines? Should GraphCodeBERT have been included as a baseline?\n3. R2's W5 (null result framed as recommendation) — does the experimental evidence actually support the \"safe alternative\" framing from a methods perspective?\n\nKeep response concise (under 400 words). End with whether R2's findings change your rating.\n","model":"sonnet"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6518,"cache_read_input_tokens":52325,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6518},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"701cd20f-444e-4df2-ad65-972d8dd61bb4","uuid":"f9397357-85a4-48dc-b253-2be0a15b505c"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"adb347aa746d122e2","tool_use_id":"toolu_011cXxQuv4vaBMRkfkV7Pdq1","description":"R1 cross-share on R2 findings","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (Method + Experiments scope) for a Dream paper review. You previously reviewed \"When Hard Negatives Hurt: Embedding-Distance Negative Mining Degrades Contrastive Code Retrieval\" and gave a \"Borderline (leaning weak reject)\" rating.\n\nYour key weaknesses were:\n- W1: Z% coverage collapse (40.8%→25.0%) not explained\n- W2: EmbDist re-mining every epoch is a confound (curriculum shift vs selection criterion)\n- W3: InfoNCE temperature τ=0.05 not ablated, could interact with negative hardness\n- W4: End-to-end resolve rate gap (0.5pp) is ~10× smaller than R@5 gap (5.0pp)\n- W5: Gradient redundancy is correlation (r=0.71), not actual gradient measurement\n- W6: RepoBench-R results lack error bars\n\nNow Reviewer 2 (Novelty + Related Work) has submitted their report. From your Method+Experiments perspective, provide supplementary comments on R2's findings:\n\n**R2's Weaknesses:**\n- W1: Positioning against CoCoHaNeRe insufficient — unclear if gradient redundancy mechanism is novel or restated\n- W2: \"First to use inter-procedural data-flow\" overclaimed — GraphCodeBERT uses data-flow edges in pre-training, clone detection literature not surveyed\n- W3: Reference list sparse (11 entries) — missing Kalantidis et al. 2020, Robinson et al. 2021, GraphCodeBERT, RocketQA, ADORE\n- W4: Story consistency — abstract claims domain-general mechanism but conclusion narrows to single encoder/Python\n- W5: DataFlow result (+0.7pp, p=0.42) framed as \"safe alternative\" but is statistically a null result\n\n**Your task:** From your method+experiments angle, comment on:\n1. Does R2's concern about CoCoHaNeRe (their W1) affect the methodological contribution? Is the controlled experiment design sufficient differentiation even if the mechanism was previously noted?\n2. Does R2's concern about GraphCodeBERT (their W2) impact the experimental baselines? Should GraphCodeBERT have been included as a baseline?\n3. R2's W5 (null result framed as recommendation) — does the experimental evidence actually support the \"safe alternative\" framing from a methods perspective?\n\nKeep response concise (under 400 words). End with whether R2's findings change your rating.\n","uuid":"15c51e0e-b4ce-4f83-a06f-80be1c378e8e","session_id":"701cd20f-444e-4df2-ad65-972d8dd61bb4"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 (Method + Experiments scope) for a Dream paper review. You previously reviewed \"When Hard Negatives Hurt: Embedding-Distance Negative Mining Degrades Contrastive Code Retrieval\" and gave a \"Borderline (leaning weak reject)\" rating.\n\nYour key weaknesses were:\n- W1: Z% coverage collapse (40.8%→25.0%) not explained\n- W2: EmbDist re-mining every epoch is a confound (curriculum shift vs selection criterion)\n- W3: InfoNCE temperature τ=0.05 not ablated, could interact with negative hardness\n- W4: End-to-end resolve rate gap (0.5pp) is ~10× smaller than R@5 gap (5.0pp)\n- W5: Gradient redundancy is correlation (r=0.71), not actual gradient measurement\n- W6: RepoBench-R results lack error bars\n\nNow Reviewer 2 (Novelty + Related Work) has submitted their report. From your Method+Experiments perspective, provide supplementary comments on R2's findings:\n\n**R2's Weaknesses:**\n- W1: Positioning against CoCoHaNeRe insufficient — unclear if gradient redundancy mechanism is novel or restated\n- W2: \"First to use inter-procedural data-flow\" overclaimed — GraphCodeBERT uses data-flow edges in pre-training, clone detection literature not surveyed\n- W3: Reference list sparse (11 entries) — missing Kalantidis et al. 2020, Robinson et al. 2021, GraphCodeBERT, RocketQA, ADORE\n- W4: Story consistency — abstract claims domain-general mechanism but conclusion narrows to single encoder/Python\n- W5: DataFlow result (+0.7pp, p=0.42) framed as \"safe alternative\" but is statistically a null result\n\n**Your task:** From your method+experiments angle, comment on:\n1. Does R2's concern about CoCoHaNeRe (their W1) affect the methodological contribution? Is the controlled experiment design sufficient differentiation even if the mechanism was previously noted?\n2. Does R2's concern about GraphCodeBERT (their W2) impact the experimental baselines? Should GraphCodeBERT have been included as a baseline?\n3. R2's W5 (null result framed as recommendation) — does the experimental evidence actually support the \"safe alternative\" framing from a methods perspective?\n\nKeep response concise (under 400 words). End with whether R2's findings change your rating.\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011cXxQuv4vaBMRkfkV7Pdq1","session_id":"701cd20f-444e-4df2-ad65-972d8dd61bb4","uuid":"05e4ebb5-6590-4315-8303-5ef0d072ec8e","timestamp":"2026-05-12T21:17:28.367Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01QyG6mmYVZhPGr5LjUTxyLk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018tnEdEv1thR24Zzbgu6NSr","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2 cross-share on R1 findings","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (Novelty + Related Work scope) for a Dream paper review. You previously reviewed \"When Hard Negatives Hurt: Embedding-Distance Negative Mining Degrades Contrastive Code Retrieval\" and gave a \"Borderline (lean reject)\" rating.\n\nYour key weaknesses were:\n- W1: CoCoHaNeRe differentiation insufficient\n- W2: \"First to use inter-procedural data-flow\" overclaimed\n- W3: Sparse references (11 entries), missing key citations\n- W4: Story consistency — mechanism's implicit generality vs narrow conclusion\n- W5: Null result (p=0.42) framed as \"safe alternative\"\n\nNow Reviewer 1 (Method + Experiments) has submitted their report. From your novelty+story perspective, provide supplementary comments on R1's findings:\n\n**R1's Weaknesses:**\n- W1: Z% coverage collapse from 40.8% to 25.0% at scale not explained\n- W2: EmbDist re-mining every epoch is a confound — could be curriculum shift instability not the selection criterion itself\n- W3: InfoNCE temperature τ=0.05 not ablated — extremely low temp interacts with negative hardness, making conclusions potentially regime-specific\n- W4: End-to-end resolve rates show only 0.5pp gap vs 5.0pp retrieval gap — practical significance questioned\n- W5: \"Gradient redundancy\" is correlation (r=0.71 between cosine sim and harm), not actual gradient measurement\n- W6: RepoBench-R results lack error bars\n\n**Your task:** From your novelty+story angle, comment on:\n1. R1's W2 (re-mining confound) — if the harm is partially from training instability rather than embedding-distance selection, does this affect the novelty of the \"gradient redundancy\" mechanism claim?\n2. R1's W3 (temperature regime) — if the finding is τ-specific, does the story's generalized framing (\"harder negatives are not always better\") become overclaimed?\n3. R1's W4 (end-to-end attenuation) — does the 10× gap reduction between retrieval and resolve rates undermine the paper's framing as a \"cautionary result for the contrastive code retrieval community\"?\n\nKeep response concise (under 400 words). End with whether R1's findings change your rating.\n","model":"sonnet"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6518,"cache_read_input_tokens":52325,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6518},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"701cd20f-444e-4df2-ad65-972d8dd61bb4","uuid":"e74c607d-ecde-4648-b944-6f0f235e3456"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a8e6618c05f1154c8","tool_use_id":"toolu_018tnEdEv1thR24Zzbgu6NSr","description":"R2 cross-share on R1 findings","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (Novelty + Related Work scope) for a Dream paper review. You previously reviewed \"When Hard Negatives Hurt: Embedding-Distance Negative Mining Degrades Contrastive Code Retrieval\" and gave a \"Borderline (lean reject)\" rating.\n\nYour key weaknesses were:\n- W1: CoCoHaNeRe differentiation insufficient\n- W2: \"First to use inter-procedural data-flow\" overclaimed\n- W3: Sparse references (11 entries), missing key citations\n- W4: Story consistency — mechanism's implicit generality vs narrow conclusion\n- W5: Null result (p=0.42) framed as \"safe alternative\"\n\nNow Reviewer 1 (Method + Experiments) has submitted their report. From your novelty+story perspective, provide supplementary comments on R1's findings:\n\n**R1's Weaknesses:**\n- W1: Z% coverage collapse from 40.8% to 25.0% at scale not explained\n- W2: EmbDist re-mining every epoch is a confound — could be curriculum shift instability not the selection criterion itself\n- W3: InfoNCE temperature τ=0.05 not ablated — extremely low temp interacts with negative hardness, making conclusions potentially regime-specific\n- W4: End-to-end resolve rates show only 0.5pp gap vs 5.0pp retrieval gap — practical significance questioned\n- W5: \"Gradient redundancy\" is correlation (r=0.71 between cosine sim and harm), not actual gradient measurement\n- W6: RepoBench-R results lack error bars\n\n**Your task:** From your novelty+story angle, comment on:\n1. R1's W2 (re-mining confound) — if the harm is partially from training instability rather than embedding-distance selection, does this affect the novelty of the \"gradient redundancy\" mechanism claim?\n2. R1's W3 (temperature regime) — if the finding is τ-specific, does the story's generalized framing (\"harder negatives are not always better\") become overclaimed?\n3. R1's W4 (end-to-end attenuation) — does the 10× gap reduction between retrieval and resolve rates undermine the paper's framing as a \"cautionary result for the contrastive code retrieval community\"?\n\nKeep response concise (under 400 words). End with whether R1's findings change your rating.\n","uuid":"6e4ea8c7-7f13-4f79-9574-85d82c74982a","session_id":"701cd20f-444e-4df2-ad65-972d8dd61bb4"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (Novelty + Related Work scope) for a Dream paper review. You previously reviewed \"When Hard Negatives Hurt: Embedding-Distance Negative Mining Degrades Contrastive Code Retrieval\" and gave a \"Borderline (lean reject)\" rating.\n\nYour key weaknesses were:\n- W1: CoCoHaNeRe differentiation insufficient\n- W2: \"First to use inter-procedural data-flow\" overclaimed\n- W3: Sparse references (11 entries), missing key citations\n- W4: Story consistency — mechanism's implicit generality vs narrow conclusion\n- W5: Null result (p=0.42) framed as \"safe alternative\"\n\nNow Reviewer 1 (Method + Experiments) has submitted their report. From your novelty+story perspective, provide supplementary comments on R1's findings:\n\n**R1's Weaknesses:**\n- W1: Z% coverage collapse from 40.8% to 25.0% at scale not explained\n- W2: EmbDist re-mining every epoch is a confound — could be curriculum shift instability not the selection criterion itself\n- W3: InfoNCE temperature τ=0.05 not ablated — extremely low temp interacts with negative hardness, making conclusions potentially regime-specific\n- W4: End-to-end resolve rates show only 0.5pp gap vs 5.0pp retrieval gap — practical significance questioned\n- W5: \"Gradient redundancy\" is correlation (r=0.71 between cosine sim and harm), not actual gradient measurement\n- W6: RepoBench-R results lack error bars\n\n**Your task:** From your novelty+story angle, comment on:\n1. R1's W2 (re-mining confound) — if the harm is partially from training instability rather than embedding-distance selection, does this affect the novelty of the \"gradient redundancy\" mechanism claim?\n2. R1's W3 (temperature regime) — if the finding is τ-specific, does the story's generalized framing (\"harder negatives are not always better\") become overclaimed?\n3. R1's W4 (end-to-end attenuation) — does the 10× gap reduction between retrieval and resolve rates undermine the paper's framing as a \"cautionary result for the contrastive code retrieval community\"?\n\nKeep response concise (under 400 words). End with whether R1's findings change your rating.\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018tnEdEv1thR24Zzbgu6NSr","session_id":"701cd20f-444e-4df2-ad65-972d8dd61bb4","uuid":"af13b087-9f84-4862-a238-c9b7ee165251","timestamp":"2026-05-12T21:17:38.402Z"}
